\title{OPT-I: the budget of the eager probes, and declarations that repeat an earlier one}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{driver}
\tag{loader}
\tag{performance}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{benchmark}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Optimisation package "OPT-I" of the second wave after the first release, 2026-11-25 (12:11 to 19:05 UTC). It takes up two of the proposals of [the comparison of RLVM with gruszka unit by unit](rep-OPT-E), sections 5.1, 4.3 and 6: the tick budget of the probes of [eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction), and the declarations of an export that repeat an earlier declaration word for word. Both were treated as separate changes with separate evidence: a private copy of the tracked sources per change, built with \src{scripts/build.sh} as a development distribution with the executable, against a pristine twin of the main tree of 12:11 UTC, which is the merged tree of [the integration of the first wave](rep-INT8). Nothing was published; the changes are patches. No Oracle GraalVM was used. The official checker (leanchecker of Lean v4.36.0) ran only inside the project's Docker image.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Change 1, the default budget of an eager probe from 2^22 to 2^17 ticks: GO.} The sizes of all probes of the eight corpora fall into three groups (below 1,600 ticks; 34,096 to 63,654; 458,881 and more), the same with one worker, with 8 workers and with sessions of one declaration; 2^17 lies in the upper gap, 2.06 times above the largest probe that completes and 3.5 times below the smallest it cuts. It changes exactly five units: four of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic} on cslib and on Mathlib go from accepted by the machine to declined by it and decided by the reference engine in 15,760 steps, and con-leche's one cut probe is cut earlier; -7.85 M ticks on cslib (3.64 \%) and Mathlib (1.37 \%), -4.06 M on con-leche (5.74 \%); every counter and every unit of the other five corpora, and every other unit of these three, is identical. Every gate passes with the counts of the base. The guard holds on the executable on a calm host: geometric mean of the eight warm passes 1.855 to 1.830 s, -1.4 \%, no corpus slower, cslib -3.8 \%, con-leche -3.2 \%, Mathlib -1.8 \%; on the JVM launcher it is flat (1.558 and 1.557 s, largest loss +1.1 \%). Peak memory falls by 360 to 520 MiB on the three corpora (sections 2.2 to 2.5).}
\li{\strong{Change 2, a declaration that repeats an accepted one word for word is admitted and not checked again: GO, with one thing the maintainer has to accept.} 5.0 to 12.4 \% of the units of the eight corpora are such repeats (59,900 of Mathlib's 721,845) and carry 1.3 to 3.7 \% of the machine's ticks. The loader marks them by the identity of their type and value records, the driver skips the engine when the original is ACCEPTED, the admission of the repeat still runs, the reference engine as primary engine and so the strict policy never skip. The official checker gives a repeat the verdict of its original on 1,193 of 1,193 hostile files made for the question; the reference engine accepts every skipped unit of the eight corpora in a differential run; every gate passes with the counts of the base; with nothing kept between declarations the ticks of the skipped units disappear and nothing else changes. The guard holds on the executable on a calm host: geometric mean 1.855 to 1.829 s, -1.4 \%, every corpus faster (Mathlib 13.17 to 12.97 s); JVM launcher -0.7 \%, largest loss +1.7 \%; the arena's instruction count on Mathlib 1,462.1 to 1,449.6 G (sections 3.5 to 3.8).}
\li{\strong{The one thing.} For the machine the second check does not always say what the first said: one hostile row of kind T is accepted by the default configuration through its tabling of shared terms, and the copy of the same two records, checked, is rejected. The base therefore rejects that row with a repeat appended, at the repeat, which is the official verdict; with the skip it is a tolerated accept. No underivable statement is accepted, on this file or by the skip in general: the statement of a repeat is the statement of its original. It is the only verdict that differs on 1,648 hostile files, and it made one rule necessary so that no status depends on the schedule (sections 3.4 and 3.5).}
\li{\strong{The level fix.} All of this was built and gated before the repair of the level store's instantiation caches; the single-run gates and the counters were repeated through the jars of private builds that carry the repair, with the same results (section 1.1).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. Conditions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Builds.} Three development distributions from three private copies of the same sources: the base, the base with change 1, the base with change 2. Each is the executable (GraalVM Community Edition native image, \code{-O3}, serial collector, the inline lists of the machine) and the JVM launcher with the jars. The default configuration throughout: the machine as primary engine with its default switch set, policy practical, a run without any \code{-X} option unless a row names one.}
\li{\strong{Counts} are from one worker in file order with batch loading (\code{\verb~|--threads 1 --stats -X stats-file=F -X unit-trace~}), where every counter of the machine is deterministic; the unit trace gives the ticks and the outcome of the warm attempt of every checked unit. "Sessions of one declaration" is \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,-warm~}. Count runs were made off the measurement cores; they are not timings.}
\li{\strong{Timings} are warm passes that reload the file with pipelined loading, 8 workers, \code{\verb~|--threads 8 --stats -X passes=N~} with N = 10 on Init and Std, 8 on cedar, 15 on reachability, 6 on con-leche, navier-stokes-euler and cslib, 4 on Mathlib. The three builds ran interleaved round by round inside \src{bench/quiet.py} windows on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host, one window per launcher and set of corpora; a cell is the mean over the rounds of the median later pass of a process, and for passes under 0.5 s the mean over the rounds of the mean later pass, as [the rule for a default](dec-YP0N) says. The JVM launcher ran with its heap line for the machine, \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}. Until about 17:15 UTC four other packages built, tested and counted on the other twelve cores (load average 45 to 95), and the windows of that time do not resolve 3 \%; from 17:51 UTC the other cores were calm (load average 4 to 10 beside the window), and the guard is from those windows: 6 rounds on the five short corpora, 3 on the three large ones, 18 on reachability on the JVM launcher.}
\li{\strong{Gates} ran through the launchers of the private distributions with \src{scripts/gate.sh}; the suites of the changed modules ran in the private copies.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 The level fix}

\p{While the package ran, a defect of the base was found and repaired: an instantiation cache of the level store could outlive a reset ([the report of that investigation](rep-INV-1)). It is older than both changes, and base and candidates of this report have the same level store, so every comparison here is between like and like. Everything in sections 2 and 3 was built and run \em{without} the fix. Then the three private copies were given the two patches of the fix as posted on 2026-11-25 15:36 UTC (the base so built has byte-identical jars to the build of the investigation, and the two patched source files are those of the main tree after the fix was published), built as jars with the JVM launcher, and the single-run gates and the counters were repeated on them:}

\table{
\tr{\th{On the builds with the level fix, through the jars}\th{Change 1}\th{Change 2}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile~}}\td{230 of 230; 17,775 mutants SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,739, declined 2,034, the same 2 at the limit; hostile 1,124 agree, 68 tolerated, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined}\td{the same counts}}
\tr{\td{the two mutants at the limit, alone}\td{rejected}\td{rejected}}
\tr{\td{the 1,648 hostile files with repeats}\td{-}\td{1,570 agree, 73 tolerated, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 5 declined; with \code{\verb~|repeats=check~} 1,571 and 72}}
\tr{\td{the eight corpora with 8 and 4 workers, JVM launcher}\td{16 of 16 accept}\td{16 of 16 accept}}
\tr{\td{one worker, every count and every unit against the base with the fix}\td{the differences of section 2.3 and no other: 1, 4 and 4 units on con-leche, cslib and Mathlib}\td{with \code{\verb~|repeats=check~} identical on all eight corpora; with the skip the ticks of section 3.7}}
}

\p{The base with the fix has the counters of the base without it on all eight corpora with one worker (ticks, records, declines, every unit), so the tables of sections 2.3 and 3.7 stand for both. \strong{Not repeated with the fix:} the executables (not rebuilt), the suites of the modules, the invariance suite, the differential runs, the arena's procedure, and the timings, which are comparisons between builds with the same level store.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Change 1: the default budget of the eager probes, 2^22 to 2^17 ticks}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 What a probe is, and what the budget does}

\p{With the switch \code{eager} the plain phase of a declaration that may defer eager comparisons runs, at the two points where the official kernel answers differently under eager reduction, what the eager branch would do, to learn whether the two modes agree there ([eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction)). The official plain procedure never starts that evaluation, so the work of a probe is work the reference engine does not have. A probe is a speculative segment under a tick budget, \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe=N~} ([the changelog entry](log-P6FV)): a probe that is cut leaves its point undecided, no further probe is started in the declaration, and the declaration is declined when it enters eager reduction, so that the reference engine decides it. The budget was 2^22 ticks, the largest probe that completes on the eight corpora with a factor of 1.5 to spare, "so that the machine declines nothing it decides". The comparison with gruszka showed what that costs: four proofs of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic} spend 0.98 to 3.2 M ticks each in probes that complete, where the reference engine decides the four in 15,760 steps.}

\p{The change is one constant, \code{MachineEngine.DEFAULT_EAGER_PROBE}, and the test that pins it. It is cost only in the verdict: a unit whose probe is cut takes the path the switch already has, and the engine that follows the official order decides it. It is not cost only in the counters: such a unit becomes a decline of the machine.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 The sizes of the probes}

\p{A private build of the machine printed every probe of 64 ticks and more with its own ticks (the ticks its speculative segment used; the statistic \code{machine.eager.probeMaxTicks} sees only outermost probes that complete, and misses a probe inside a declaration that was restarted). One worker in file order, budget 2^22:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{probes of 64 ticks and more}\th{of them below 4,096 ticks: largest}\th{34,096 to 63,654 ticks}\th{458,881 ticks and more}\th{cut at 2^22}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{79}\td{75: 248}\td{4}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{261}\td{257: 318}\td{4}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{463}\td{459: 370}\td{4}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{3}\td{3: 102}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{54}\td{53: 193}\td{0}\td{0}\td{1}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{72}\td{72: 396}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{951}\td{941: 812}\td{4}\td{6}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{2,039}\td{2,027: 1,593}\td{6}\td{6}\td{0}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Three groups, with two gaps.} Below 1,600 ticks: every probe but thirteen. Between 34,096 and 63,654 ticks: six probes in six units, four of \code{Init.Data.Char.Ordinal} (\code{\verb~|Char.ofOrdinal._proof_5~} 34,110, \code{\verb~|Char.ordinal_ofOrdinal._proof_1_1~} 34,120, \code{\verb~|Char.succ?_eq._proof_1_10~} 62,850, \code{\verb~|Char.succ?_eq._proof_1_9~} 63,654), which every corpus built on Init contains, and two of \code{Batteries.Data.Fin.Coding} on Mathlib (34,096 and 34,106). At 458,881 ticks and more: six probes in the four units of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic} on cslib and Mathlib (\code{\verb~|Char.all._proof_1~} 974,109; \code{\verb~|Char.any._proof_1~} 974,142; \code{\verb~|Char.of_any_eq_false_aux._proof_1_15~} 2,752,577 and then 458,881; \code{\verb~|._proof_1_18~} 2,752,589 and then 458,891), and the probe of con-leche's \code{ConLeche.lpOfData_pack} that never completes (finding INT7-F1).}
\li{\strong{The sizes do not depend on the schedule.} With 8 workers (Init, Std, cedar, reachability, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler) and with sessions of one declaration (the same and cslib) the six probes of the middle group have exactly the ticks above, and the large ones differ by at most 22 ticks. A probe evaluates in a segment of its own and takes next to nothing from the session.}
\li{\strong{The choice: 2^17 = 131,072 ticks.} Every budget between 63,655 and 458,880 ticks cuts the same seven probes and completes the same others, in every schedule measured. The budget costs its own ticks once per unit that is cut, so the three candidates 2^16, 2^17 and 2^18 differ by 0.26 M and 0.52 M ticks on cslib and on Mathlib (0.13 and 0.25 \% of cslib's ticks) and by 0.07 and 0.13 M on con-leche, which no timing resolves (section 2.4). They differ in their margin: 2^16 = 65,536 is 3.0 \% above the largest probe that completes, so a change of the machine that moves a tick count by a thirtieth would send four units of Init, in every corpus, to the reference engine; 2^18 is 4.1 times above it and 1.75 times below the smallest probe it has to cut. \strong{2^17 is 2.06 times above the largest probe that is worth completing (63,654) and 3.5 times below the smallest that is not (458,881)}, close to the middle of the gap, which is at 170,900 ticks.}
\li{The lower gap, between 1,600 and 34,096 ticks, was not examined: a budget there would also hand the six units of the middle group to the reference engine. The count of [the comparison with gruszka](rep-OPT-E) at 2^12 says -0.3 to -0.6 \% of the machine's ticks on Init, Std and cedar for 41,651 steps of the reference engine; whether that is a gain in time is not known.}
}
}

