}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The executable: the guard holds.} The geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 1.855 to 1.830 s, -1.4 \%, and no corpus is slower. Where a probe is cut: cslib 4.771 to 4.589 s, -3.8 \%, every round below every round of the base; con-leche 1.414 to 1.369 s, -3.2 \%; Mathlib 13.172 to 12.937 s, -1.8 \%, in each round below the base's round. On the five corpora where the change executes the same ticks the cells are within -1.2 and -0.1 \% of the base, which is the scatter of these windows. The process CPU of a pass falls by 6.3 \% on con-leche, 3.2 \% on cslib and 1.2 \% on Mathlib and is within 1.3 \% of the base's on the others; over the eight corpora -1.5 \%.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher: no regression, and no gain.} Geometric mean 1.558 and 1.557 s; the largest loss is con-leche, +1.1 \% (with 2.9 \% less CPU), the largest gain Init, -1.4 \%, a corpus on which nothing changes. cslib -0.8 \%, Mathlib 0.0 \%. On the JVM the four units cost about half of what they cost in the executable to begin with (0.68 against 1.33 s on cslib in the unit traces of [the profile](rep-P11)), and a JVM process differs from the next by more than that: two samples of the base on reachability, twelve rounds each in one window, differ by 1.1 \%.}
\li{\strong{Memory.} The peak resident set falls where a probe is cut, because a probe of millions of ticks is one session of that size: in the executable con-leche 1,358 to 927 MiB, cslib 2,127 to 1,610 MiB, Mathlib 4,384 to 4,026 MiB; on the JVM launcher cslib 2,847 to 2,444 MiB and Mathlib 5,094 to 4,808 MiB.}
\li{\strong{Instructions.} In the arena's count the change is -2.90 \% on con-leche, -1.63 \% on cslib and -0.55 \% on Mathlib (1,462.1 to 1,454.0 G), and within 0.1 \% on everything else (table below).}
}

