\p{The two mutants are those that every gate of this wave has seen at the limit on a loaded host: each needs 30 s of CPU with the changed literal, and the efficiency cores were oversubscribed five times over.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.6 What the switch reference and the notes on eager reduction must say}

\p{This report does not edit them. With the change in the main tree:}

\ul{
\li{[The switch reference](design-switches-document): in the paragraph "Related settings", \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe=N~} has the default 2^17, not 2^22; the row of the switch set \code{Op.SW_DEFAULT} says "with the probe budget \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-probe~} = 2^17"; the paragraph on the probes under the table of named switches gets the three groups of probe sizes of section 2.2, the four units of cslib and Mathlib that the machine now declines, and the sentence that the budget lies in a gap of the sizes, 2.06 times above the largest probe that completes and 3.5 times below the smallest that is cut, where 2^22 had been 1.5 times above the largest probe of any size.}
\li{[Eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction): "a budget of 2^22 ticks" becomes 2^17, and "on cslib and Mathlib no probe is cut" becomes "four units of \code{Batteries.Data.Char.Basic} are cut, declined and decided by the reference engine in 15,760 steps".}
\li{[The decision on the default set](dec-YP0N) names the budget of 2^22 in its decision paragraph; it is a decision and stays as written, with a link to the changelog entry of this change.}
\li{The statistics \code{machine.eager.probeBudget}, \code{probeCuts}, \code{probeMaxTicks} and \code{declined.probe} say after any run which units were cut.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Change 2: a declaration that repeats an accepted one is not checked again}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 What a repeat is, and how many there are}

\p{The exporter writes many declarations more than once under different names: auxiliary proofs, equation lemmas, matchers and compiler auxiliaries that the elaborator emits again. Such a record names the \em{same expression records} as type and as value as an earlier record of the same kind with the same universe parameters. A unit is a \em{repeat} here if it is a definition, a theorem or an opaque, and an earlier unit of the file has the same kind, the same list of universe-parameter names, the same reducibility hint (definitions) and the same two record ordinals as type and as value; its \em{original} is the first such unit. Counted by the loader of the change, and the ticks of the repeats in the base (one worker, file order, warm sessions):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{units}\th{repeats}\th{their ticks in the base}\th{largest repeat, ticks}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{58,547}\td{5,948 (10.2 \%)}\td{1,236,211 (3.58 \% of 34,490,709)}\td{16,120}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{101,146}\td{8,661 (8.6 \%)}\td{1,833,913 (3.22 \% of 56,957,035)}\td{22,653}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{146,058}\td{15,429 (10.6 \%)}\td{2,827,396 (3.69 \% of 76,668,269)}\td{22,660}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{4,413}\td{220 (5.0 \%)}\td{8,175 (0.09 \% of 9,014,777)}\td{528}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{36,747}\td{4,570 (12.4 \%)}\td{2,021,517 (2.86 \% of 70,755,128)}\td{31,374}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{126,468}\td{13,331 (10.5 \%)}\td{1,974,828 (1.37 \% of 144,220,381)}\td{15,920}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{401,985}\td{30,445 (7.6 \%)}\td{4,131,989 (1.91 \% of 215,956,521)}\td{22,653}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{721,845}\td{59,900 (8.3 \%)}\td{7,492,778 (1.31 \% of 574,100,326)}\td{44,499}}
}

\p{The count of [the comparison with gruszka](rep-OPT-E) (59,923 on Mathlib, 13,336 on navier-stokes-euler, 30,466 on cslib) is this set but for 23, 5 and 21 units: the key here also has the reducibility hint of a definition. By that count three quarters of Mathlib's repeats are theorems, and a repeat is far from its original: the median distance is 233,976 units on Mathlib.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 The claim, and what the check of a declaration reads}

\p{\strong{Claim.} Let unit r repeat unit o: both are definitions, or both theorems, or both opaques; they have the same list of universe-parameter names and, for definitions, the same reducibility hint; and the type and the value of r are the \em{same expression records} of the export as those of o. Let o come before r in the file and in admission order. If the check of o ends in ACCEPT and the admission of r passes, the check of r ends in ACCEPT, for the official checker and for both engines of RLVM.}

\p{\strong{What the official check reads.} For a definition, theorem or opaque the official kernel does this, and nothing else: the name must not be declared yet; the universe parameters must be distinct; the type must have no free variable or metavariable; the type is inferred in check mode and must be a sort, for a theorem the sort of propositions; the value is inferred in check mode and its type compared with the declared type. Its seven caches live for one declaration, see [the caches of the reference engine against the official kernel's](design-reference-caches). So the verdict is a function of these inputs:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input of the check}\th{For a repeat}\th{Where it is decided}}
\tr{\td{the kind: definition, theorem or opaque (a theorem's type must be a proposition; an axiom has no value)}\td{the same, part of the key}\td{the loader's key}}
\tr{\td{the universe-parameter names, as a list (distinctness; every parameter the terms mention must be declared)}\td{the same list, part of the key}\td{the loader's key}}
\tr{\td{the type and the value, as terms and as objects: the official kernel tests object identity in two places, and one export record is one object}\td{the same records, so the same terms and the same objects}\td{the loader's key: record ordinals, not structural equality}}
\tr{\td{the safety (a safe declaration may not use an unsafe or partial constant)}\td{always safe: unsafe and partial records are dropped by the loader and never checked}\td{the loader}}
\tr{\td{the reducibility hint of a definition}\td{not read by the check of the definition itself, which cannot mention its own name; kept in the key all the same}\td{the loader's key}}
\tr{\td{the declaration's own name: "already declared"}\td{differs, and is checked}\td{the loader rejects a second declaration of a name; admission checks it again in O(1)}}
\tr{\td{the declaration's own name inside its type or value}\td{impossible for an accepted original: neither name is declared when the original is checked, so the original would have failed with an unknown constant}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{the constants the terms mention, and through unfolding the constants those mention: their kinds, types, values, hints, recursor rules}\td{the same constants: a constant is never replaced, and what the original could see the repeat sees (the environment only grows)}\td{admission: every constant below a root has a smaller rank; the driver asks in addition that the original's rank is smaller than the repeat's}}
\tr{\td{names the kernel makes up itself: \code{Nat}, \code{Nat.zero}, \code{Nat.succ} for a literal, the accelerated functions and \code{Bool.true}, \code{Bool.false} for their results, \code{String.ofList}, \code{List.cons}, \code{Char.ofNat} for a string literal, the quotient constants, \code{eagerReduce}}\td{a lookup of such a name that \em{fails} without an error is the one place where a larger environment could change a path. For an accepted original it does not occur: the type of a literal is the constant \code{Nat}, which some checked binder or constant type must then mention, so it is declared, with its constructors, in one unit; an accelerated function fires only where the term names it; RLVM declines a root with a literal whose prerequisite constants are not declared earlier, and declines when a guard of a special constant does not hold}\td{admission (structural checks, prelude guards)}}
\tr{\td{the position in the file: declared before use}\td{the repeat is later}\td{as above}}
\tr{\td{the "all" list of the record (the names of a mutual block)}\td{not read by the kernel for these kinds; the loader does not keep it}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{resources of the official process: the depth of its stack when the declaration is reached in its replay}\td{not modelled by RLVM for any declaration}\td{-}}
}

\p{\strong{Kinds for which the list is not closed}, and which are not part of the skip: an axiom (no value; its admission depends on its name under the axiom policy; four per corpus); inductive blocks, constructors, recursors and the quotient records (the environment pass decides them, and their check is about their names). A unit that the environment pass admits itself in dependency order never reaches an engine, with or without the skip.}

\p{\strong{RLVM's engines.} The machine reads the rank of the declaration it checks in four places, each of the form "the constant's rank is not below the rank of the declaration: not visible" (\code{Infer}, \code{Values}, \code{NatRed}); nothing else of the unit's position or name enters a judgment. A session may hold facts from earlier units, and the driver's invariant, tested by its invariance suite, is that no verdict depends on them. The reference engine's caches live for one declaration. So for both engines the outcome of r is the outcome of o. The default configuration may accept an original that the official checker rejects for a reason of its own (class (d) of [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract)): its repeat is then accepted too, with or without the skip, as the same derivable statement with the same proof.}

\p{\strong{Soundness does not rest on the claim.} If o was accepted, its value has its type in the environment of o, and every environment that extends it by further constants gives the same typing: the statement of r is the statement of o and is derivable. The claim is needed for the other direction, that the skip never accepts what a check would have rejected or declined for a reason of the official implementation; for that it was tested (section 3.5).}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 The implementation}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The loader says which unit a unit repeats.} Only it can: the value of a theorem is a proof block in the original and the promoted copy of that block's root in the first repeat, and nothing in the store links the two. At the declaration line the loader looks the declaration up in a table of the definitions, theorems and opaques read so far (\code{rlvm.load.Repeats}: an exact key of kind, id of the interned list of universe-parameter names, hint, and the two record ordinals; six ints per declaration; a declaration with a root in its own segment cannot repeat anything and is added without a search). The result goes through one new call of the loader's target, \code{LoadTarget.repeatOf(firstUnit)}, into a column of the declaration table, \code{DeclTable.repeatOf(unit)}: one int per unit in chunks that exist only where a unit repeats another, written before the unit is published. The store reads it nowhere, and every other word the loader writes is what it was. A metadata record has no ordinal of its own (the wrapped record stands for it), so a declaration whose value is a metadata record around an earlier value repeats it; under the practical policy that is what the base checks too, and the strict policy never skips.}
\li{\strong{The driver admits a repeat and does not check it.} A worker that comes to a unit with an original (\code{RunHost.unitRepeatOf}; the original must also be earlier in admission order) reads the original's status on the verdict board. If it is ACCEPTED, the unit goes through the lane's admission exactly as any unit does - the environment frontier, a recorded outcome of the environment pass, the name, the universe parameters, the roots - with a session that answers ACCEPT where the engine would have been called. Whatever the admission finds is reported through the unchanged protocol. Nothing in \code{rlvm-admit} changed.}
\li{\strong{Setting.} \code{\verb~|-X repeats=skip~} (the default) or \code{\verb~|repeats=check~}; statistics \code{load.repeatUnits}, \code{driver.repeatsSkipped} and \code{driver.repeatsAwaited}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.4 The verdict rule, the original in flight, the engines, the strict policy}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The verdict.} The verdict of a file is the status of its first unit in file order that is not accepted ([the verdict protocol](design-verdict-protocol)). A repeat is reported ACCEPT without a check only when its original is ACCEPTED, and a status is written once, so an ACCEPTED read is final. If the original is rejected or declined, the verdict is decided at or before the original: the repeat lies above the first failure and is never accepted on anybody's word.}
\li{\strong{The original in flight.} With several workers the original can be without a status when the repeat comes up: on another worker, or in another worker's claim. Then the repeat is checked as in the base, and nobody waits. That would be all if the check of a repeat always said what the check of its original says. It does not on one kind of input (section 3.5): the machine accepted the original through a shortcut that the official checker does not have, and rejects the copy. With "check if in flight" alone the status of such a repeat, and the verdict of the file, would depend on the schedule. So one rule was added: \strong{if the check of a repeat whose original had no status does not end in ACCEPT, the worker gives the rest of its claim back and waits for the original's status}; if it is ACCEPTED the unit goes through the path of a repeat, as it would have a moment later; otherwise the first failure lies at or before the original. The wait is for a smaller unit that another worker holds, so no cycle of waits exists; the worker holds no gate and its session is reset; a cancelled run ends it. It did not occur on any corpus (\code{driver.repeatsAwaited} = 0 in every run of this report); a test of the driver forces it. The status of every unit is then the same in every schedule, which the invariance suite confirms on files made for it (section 3.6).}
\li{\strong{Which engine.} Only when the primary engine is not the reference engine. With \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} every unit is checked, so the gates of the reference engine and its row in the benchmark tables keep their meaning.}
\li{\strong{The strict policy} is the reference engine alone and is not touched: every declaration is checked, as the official checker checks it, and the strict mode's reading of a file is unchanged. The loader fills the column under every policy; it is not read.}
\li{\strong{Statistics and re-checks.} A skipped unit counts as accepted in the driver's counts and has zero steps in the unit trace; it is not among the units of the engine. The re-check protocol is unchanged for every unit that is checked. In a differential run (\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}) the reference engine still checks every unit, the skipped ones included, and a difference is a decline of the run: that is how the claim was tested on the corpora.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.5 The claim, tested}

\p{\strong{The official checker.} Every row of the machine's hostile suite (1,196 exports, each attacking one shortcut, memo or cache order, with the verdict of the official checker) was rewritten with its last definition, theorem or opaque appended once more at the end of the file under a new name: the same record, a word-for-word repeat (1,193 files; three rows have no such declaration or an unsafe one). leanchecker 4.36.0 judged all of them again in Docker:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Files}\th{Official verdict}\th{Against the claim}}
\tr{\td{1,193: a row with its last declaration repeated word for word}\td{414 accept, 779 reject}\td{\strong{the verdict of the row, 1,193 of 1,193}}}
\tr{\td{414: a row that the official checker accepts, with the type and value records of its last declaration under another kind (theorem to definition 396, definition or opaque to theorem 18)}\td{402 accept, 12 reject (a theorem whose type is no proposition)}\td{why the kind is part of the key}}
\tr{\td{41 written for this change (below)}\td{13 accept, 28 reject}\td{as the author expected, 41 of 41}}
}

\p{So on the files that were built to make the official verdict depend on cache order, on the sharing of records and on object identity, the official check of a repeat gives the verdict of its original every time. The 41 files of the third row are the cases the key and the rule have to get right: a repeat of a rejected original (theorem, definition, opaque); the same records under another kind in both directions (a definition \code{\verb~|a : Nat := 2 + 3~} is fine, a theorem with the same two records is not); other universe-parameter names, none, a superset, a duplicate; the name of the original or of a prelude constant; a self reference; the same statement with another proof and the same proof under another statement; the same terms as fresh records; other reducibility hints; an unsafe original; a metadata record around the value; an original that uses a constant declared later (the official replay goes by dependency); 300 repeats directly behind a unit that takes a moment, accepted and rejected; and seven rows of the suite, five of kind T, with every theorem repeated twice.}

\p{\strong{RLVM, the default configuration, on these 1,648 files} (\src{scripts/gate-hostile.py} with a manifest of the official verdicts; a row of kind T may be accepted):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Run}\th{agree}\th{kind-T accepts tolerated}\th{wrong accept}\th{wrong reject}\th{declined}}
\tr{\td{the executable, a run without options (automatic workers)}\td{1,570}\td{73}\td{0}\td{0}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{the executable, 8 workers}\td{1,570}\td{73}\td{0}\td{0}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{the jars on the JVM, automatic workers}\td{1,570}\td{73}\td{0}\td{0}\td{5}}
\tr{\td{the executable with \code{\verb~|-X repeats=check~}: every unit checked, the behaviour of the base}\td{1,571}\td{72}\td{0}\td{0}\td{5}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{No wrong accept and no wrong reject} in any run. The five declines are files the base declines too (a string literal without its prerequisites, metadata around \code{eagerReduce}, metadata in a binder domain).}
\li{\strong{One row gets another verdict with the skip than without it}: \code{R-e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad}, the row \code{e1-sib-krule-major-redex-bad} of kind T with its theorem repeated. The official checker rejects the statement through the K rule on a major premise that is a beta-redex (finding E1-F7); the statement is derivable, and the default configuration accepts it - the row is one of the 68 tolerated accepts of the suite. Checked a second time, the same two records are \em{rejected} by the machine and then by the reference engine. The reason is where the terms live, not the session: the proof of the original is a proof block, decoded for that unit; the repeat's value is the promoted copy, a shared term of an earlier unit, which the machine's tabling gate treats as shared. With \code{\verb~|-X no-proof-blocks~} the base accepts both; without the switch \code{gated} it rejects the original. So the base rejects this file, at the repeat, and accepts the same file without the repeat; with the skip it is accepted. Both verdicts are what a row of kind T allows, and nothing underivable is accepted: the statement of a repeat is the statement of its original. But it is a verdict that changes, on a hostile input, from the official one to a tolerated one, and it is why the claim of section 3.2 holds for the machine only up to its own tolerated shortcuts.}
\li{\strong{That row is also why a wait was added} (section 3.4). Without it the verdict of this file would have depended on whether the original was decided before the repeat came up. With it the invariance suite sees one verdict in every schedule (section 3.6).}
\li{The 67 other rows of kind T with a repeat, and the five written for this change, are accepted with the skip and without it.}
}

\p{\strong{The reference engine on every skipped unit.} In a differential run (\code{\verb~|-X engine=diff~}: the machine as primary engine, the reference engine beside it on every unit, a difference declines the run) the reference engine checks each unit the driver admitted without a check. The executable of the change, 8 workers, the eight corpora:}

