\tr{\td{reachability, nothing kept between declarations}\td{9,401,140}\td{9,375,053}\td{-26,087 (-0.28 \%)}\td{220}\td{26,087 (0.28 \%)}\td{+0 in 0 units}\td{0}\td{0, 0}\td{0, 0}}
\tr{\td{con-leche, nothing kept between declarations}\td{95,980,031}\td{91,094,834}\td{-4,885,197 (-5.09 \%)}\td{4,570}\td{4,885,197 (5.09 \%)}\td{+0 in 0 units}\td{0}\td{10, 10}\td{5, 5}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{With nothing kept between declarations the ticks of the skipped units disappear and nothing else changes}: on Init, reachability and con-leche every other unit has its ticks to the tick; on Std two units and on cedar three move by one to three ticks with the sum zero. The difference of the totals is exactly the ticks of the skipped units. (Not run on navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib.)}
\li{\strong{In the default configuration the other units pay a little of it back}: a warm session no longer holds what the check of a repeat left in it, and the check facts and fact records that a repeat was the first to derive are derived by a later unit. The skipped units carried 1.3 to 3.7 \% of the ticks (0.09 \% on reachability); the totals fall by 1.1 to 3.2 \% (0.13 \% on reachability). The repeats weigh more on the small corpora than the 1.3 to 1.9 \% that had been counted on Mathlib, navier-stokes-euler and cslib.}
\li{\strong{No outcome changes}: the machine's declines and rejections and the units the reference engine decides are the same on every corpus, and no unit of a trace has another outcome.}
\li{\strong{With 8 and 4 workers} nearly every repeat is skipped: with 8 workers 97.4 to 99.3 \% of the repeats of Init, Std, cedar, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (59,425 and 59,470 of Mathlib's 59,900 in the executable and on the JVM launcher), 93 and 94 \% of con-leche's and 84 and 93 \% of the 220 of reachability; with 4 workers 98.3 to 99.4 \%, 96 to 97 \% and 90 to 91 \%. The rest came up while their original had no status and were checked; none of those checks failed (\code{driver.repeatsAwaited} = 0).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.8 Timing: the guard}

\p{The tables are those of section 2.4: the windows measured the base and both changes together. On the calm host:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The executable: the guard holds.} The geometric mean of the eight warm passes goes from 1.855 to 1.829 s, -1.4 \%, and every corpus is faster: Init -1.3 \%, Std -1.8 \%, cedar -1.8 \%, reachability -1.1 \%, con-leche -1.8 \%, navier-stokes-euler -1.0 \%, cslib -0.7 \%, Mathlib -1.6 \% (13.172 to 12.967 s). On Init, cedar, cslib and Mathlib every round is below the base's round, on cslib and Mathlib below every round of the base. The process CPU of a pass falls on every corpus, by 0.7 to 2.3 \%, -1.6 \% over the eight.}
\li{\strong{The JVM launcher: no regression.} Geometric mean 1.558 to 1.547 s, -0.7 \%; Init -3.2 \%, Mathlib -1.4 \% (11.065 to 10.911 s), cedar -0.8 \%, navier-stokes-euler -0.7 \%, Std -0.6 \%, cslib -0.3 \%, reachability +0.1 \% over 18 rounds, con-leche +1.7 \%, the largest loss. The first six rounds of reachability alone read +3.6 \%; twelve more rounds in a window with a second sample of the base read -1.6 \% for the change and -1.1 \% for the base against itself.}
\li{\strong{Instructions.} In the arena's count every corpus needs fewer instructions: Init -2.39 \%, Std -2.06 \%, cedar -2.40 \%, con-leche -1.53 \%, cslib -1.23 \%, Mathlib -0.86 \% (1,462.1 to 1,449.6 G), navier-stokes-euler -0.58 \%, reachability -0.16 \%; the 262 small tests together +0.07 \% (table in section 2.4). The loader's table is in these counts.}
\li{\strong{Memory.} The peak resident set of a pass is the base's within 3 \% on every corpus (Mathlib 4,384 and 4,490 MiB in the executable, 5,094 and 5,145 MiB on the JVM launcher).}
}

